{
  "title": "Pickaxe Mountain revives the Kahuta question: Could India have crippled Pakistan's nuclear leap?",
  "publication_date": "2026-07-26T11:57:00Z",
  "summary": "The article revisits whether India could have struck Pakistan's Kahuta nuclear facility. Kahuta was within range of Indian fighters, but strategic concerns \u2014 notably the risk of a retaliatory radiological strike on Mumbai's Trombay \u2014 led India to refrain. Experts argue that the restraint preserved India's international standing and provided longer-term diplomatic advantages over a risky pre-emptive attack.",
  "content": "Kahuta is located at less than 150 KM from some of the forward bases of the IAF, which would be well within the reach of MiG21/23 and freshly inducted Jaguar aircraft. While it was physically within reach, it\u2019s the strategic consideration that weighed heavily against the attack.\n\nAn attack on Kahuta promised a tactical setback for Pakistan, but a retaliatory strike on Trombay threatened a radiological disaster for Mumbai\u2014a price India chose not to pay.\n\nIt\u2019s difficult to say what India could have achieved by attacking this facility. It was recovering from the 1971 Ops and on path to development. In my considered opinion the restraint exercised by the government paid India much greater dividends, which could be encashed at a later date through the image of a responsible nation, than the attack would have.\n\nRussia\n\nUSA\n\nUSA\n\nRussia\n\nFrance\n\nIndia\n\nN. Korea\n\nRussia\n\nChina\n\nIndia\n\nIndia\n\nIndia\n\nIndia\n\nIndia\n\nIndia\n\nIndia\n\nIndia\n\nIndia\n\nIndia\n\nIsrael\n\nIsrael\n\nIsrael\n\nIsrael\n\nIsrael\n\nIsrael\n\nIsrael\n\nIsrael\n\nIsrael\n\nIsrael\n\nIsrael\n\nUSA\n\nUSA\n\nUSA\n\nUSA\n\nUSA\n\nUSA\n\nUSA\n\nUSA\n\nIran\n\nIran\n\nIran\n\nIran\n\nIran\n\nIran\n\nIran\n\nIran\n\nIran\n\nIran\n\nIran\n\nIran\n\nRussia",
